course: dictionary
lesson: 14149
title: Words with more than one meaning
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one to use. The word misplaced is a good example. «I have misplaced my reading book again.» Here it means you put the book somewhere and now cannot find it, so it is lost for a while.
explain: Misplaced can also describe something in the wrong place. «The misplaced comma changed the whole meaning of the sentence.» The comma was not where it should be, so the sentence said something different. Ask yourself: is something lost, or just in the wrong spot?
explain: Misplaced can describe trust or confidence too. «She showed misplaced trust when she gave her password to a stranger.» The trust went to the wrong person and was not deserved. The clues in the sentence, like password and stranger, help you choose the right meaning.
fact: Misplace is a verb: I misplace, he misplaces, I have misplaced, I am misplacing.
fact: «Misplaced» means lost for a while, or put in the wrong place.
fact: «Misplaced trust» means trust given to the wrong person, or trust that is not deserved.
fact: In maths and writing, a misplaced decimal point or comma can change the meaning of a number.
fact: A good check is to swap in "lost" or "in the wrong place" and see if the sentence still makes sense.
exercise: «I have misplaced my reading book again.» What does misplaced mean here? | Put in the right place on the shelf | Read aloud to the class | Broken into pieces | *Lost for a while, so I can't find it
exercise: «The decimal point was misplaced: 3.45 was written as 34.5.» Why is the number wrong? | *The point is in the wrong place, which changes the number | The point was too big to read | The number was written in pencil | Decimal points are not used in maths
exercise: Nadia says «misplaced trust» means her keys are lost. Is she right? | No, it means trust that is very loud | Yes, it means happy trust | *No, it means trust given to the wrong person or not deserved | Yes, trust can be lost in the house
test: «The vase was on the wrong shelf, a misplaced ornament.» What does misplaced mean? | Lost for ever | Made of glass | *Put in the wrong place | Very expensive
test: «She had misplaced confidence in the broken bridge.» What does this suggest? | Confidence that was lost in the post | *Confidence that was not deserved | Confidence in building bridges | Confidence that was made of gold
exam: Which phrase means trust given to the wrong person or not deserved? | open trust | misplaced decimal point | *misplaced trust | misplaced keys
